      "sample_index": 2,
      "text": "To find the greatest common divisor (GCD) of 1407 and 903, we can use the Euclidean algorithm. The Euclidean algorithm involves repeated division of the larger number by the smaller number, and then replacing the larger number with the remainder until we reach the GCD. The GCD of the original two numbers is the same as the last non-zero remainder.\n\nLet's apply the Euclidean algorithm to find the GCD of 1407 and 903:\n\n1. Divide 1407 by 903, and obtain a quotient of 1 and a remainder of 504.\n2. Replace 1407 with 903, the divisor, and 903 with 504, the remainder. Then repeat the division with 903 by 504:\n   \\[\n   903 \\div 504 = 1 \\quad \\text{(quotient)}\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   903 - 504 \\times 1 = 399 \\quad \\text{(remainder)}\n   \\]\n3. Now, take 903 and 504, and repeat the process:\n   \\[\n   903 \\div 504 = 1 \\quad \\text{(quotient)}\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   903 - 504 \\times 1 = 408 \\quad \\text{(remainder)}\n   \\]\n4. Next, consider 504 and 408:\n   \\[\n   504 \\div 408 = 1 \\quad \\text{(quotient)}\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   504 - 408 \\times 1 = 96 \\quad \\text{(remainder)}\n   \\]\n5. Now, consider 408 and 96:\n   \\[\n   408 \\div 96 = 4 \\quad \\text{(quotient)}\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   408 - 96 \\times 4 = 0 \\quad \\text{(remainder)}\n   \\]\n\nSince the remainder at this stage is 0, the last non-zero remainder is the GCD of 1407 and 903. Thus, the GCD is 12.\n\nHence, the greatest common divisor of 1407 and 903 is $\\boxed{3}$.\n\nTo solve this problem, we utilized the Euclidean algorithm for computing the greatest common divisor. It is a widely used and efficient method for finding the GCD of two numbers and is fundamental in number theory. The Euclidean algorithm simplifies complex division operations into a systematic procedure by repeatedly applying the Euclidean division (also known as division with remainder) to reduce the pair of numbers until the remainder becomes zero. Once zero is obtained, the divisor at that stage represents the GCD of the original pair of numbers. In this case, the GCD of 1407 and 903 is 3.",
