      "text": "To find the greatest common divisor (GCD) of 1407 and 903, we can use the Euclidean algorithm.\n\nFirst, we divide 1407 by 903 and find the remainder:\n\n1407 ÷ 903 = 1, with a remainder of 504.\n\nNext, we divide 903 by 504:\n\n903 ÷ 504 = 1, with a remainder of 399.\n\nNow, we divide 504 by 399:\n\n504 ÷ 399 = 1, with a remainder of 105.\n\nNext, we divide 399 by 105:\n\n399 ÷ 105 = 3, with a remainder of 94.\n\nThen, we divide 105 by 94:\n\n105 ÷ 94 = 1, with a remainder of 11.\n\nFinally, we divide 94 by 11:\n\n94 ÷ 11 = 8, with a remainder of 6.\n\nWe now have a remainder of 6, which is a smaller number than the remainder we obtained before. We can continue this process until we reach a remainder of 0. This last non-zero remainder will be the GCD of 1407 and 903.\n\nNow, we divide 94 by 6:\n\n94 ÷ 6 = 15, with a remainder of 4.\n\nNext, we divide 6 by 4:\n\n6 ÷ 4 = 1, with a remainder of 2.\n\nFinally, we divide 4 by 2:\n\n4 ÷ 2 = 2, with a remainder of 0.\n\nSince we have reached a remainder of 0, the last non-zero remainder, which is 2, is the greatest common divisor of 1407 and 903.",
